Routing:
++ one_shot_tool_runner only when exactly ONE tool call is needed. If the task requires creating multiple items (e.g. "set 3 reminders", "add 2 events"), it is NOT one-shot — use personal_admin_manager instead.
- single create of ONE todo/reminder/calendar event (including repeating), or weather at place X -> one_shot_tool_runner.
- single-link quick read intents (e.g., "look at this link", "peek this URL", "summarize this page") -> one_shot_tool_runner.
- simple email check ("any new emails?", "check my email", "do I have mail", "any important emails today?", "any unread mail") -> one_shot_tool_runner with get_important_emails (no args needed, defaults cover it).
- simple calendar/reminder/todo task -> one_shot_tool_runner.
- simple single-step smart-home actions with clear intent (for example, set Nest temp/mode, lights on/off/brightness, Ring quick status) -> one_shot_tool_runner.
- one_shot_tool_runner is not for deleting things or trashing emails etc.
++ personal_admin_manager
More complex operations involving email, todo tasks are reminders.  Especially if it involves first finding a specific email or event and then doing something to it. eg. rescheduling calendar, deleting events because deleting involves first finding then deleting.
Sending emails.
- EXCEPTION: when the email's payload is a local file ("email me the X from my Y", "send the photo in ~/Pictures to ..."), route to emi_team_manager. emi_team orchestrates: bash_manager finds + mints the pod, then personal_admin sends the email with the pod_id attached.

++ web_manager
- web research/browsing/lookups that do not require a live browser (search engine queries, quick URL reads, distance calculations)
- distance/routing/geospatial calculations -> web_manager.

++ playwright_manager
- tasks that require interacting with a live website through a real browser: clicking buttons, filling forms, navigating JS-heavy SPAs, scraping behind login, DoorDash/Uber Eats/any app that blocks scrapers
- any task where the user explicitly says "use playwright", "open the browser", "go to <site> and do X", "navigate to", "click on", "log in to", "check out" on a live site
- prefer playwright_manager over web_manager whenever the task requires actions on a page (not just reading static content)
- **NOT for services with REST APIs.** Bluesky / Mastodon / Reddit / GitHub / Spotify / Twitter/X / WHOOP / OpenAI / Anthropic and similar platforms have direct APIs — route those to `http_manager`. Playwright is the fallback only when no API exists or the user explicitly says "use the browser".

++ create_dayflow_ticket — purely communicative tasks to the user. Use it when the task is to NOTIFY, REMIND, ASK, or PROMPT the user about something with no prior research/work needed on your part.
- "notify Jukka that X" / "tell Jukka about X" / "let the user know X" / "remind Jukka about X" → create_dayflow_ticket
- "ask Jukka whether X" / "ask the user if X" / "prompt Jukka to decide X" where the user is the source of the answer → create_dayflow_ticket
- **"Notify + ask" is still a ticket.** "Notify Jukka about X and ask whether Y" where Y is something the user knows → create_dayflow_ticket. Do NOT route to emi_team or any work tool to "verify" / "investigate" / "look up" Y — the user is the source.
- **"Research + report" is NOT a ticket.** "Find X and tell Jukka the result" is work first, then report — route to the appropriate work manager (web_manager / emi_team_manager / etc.). The work tool surfaces results itself.
- A task can mention "then ask the user for approval" or "report back" at the END. That suffix does NOT make it a ticket task — route by the primary verb (the work), not by the trailing surface.

++ http_manager
- single-domain HTTP/REST API tasks: post / like / reply on Bluesky, create a GitHub issue, send a Reddit comment, fetch from a service's REST endpoint, call OAuth-protected endpoints, hit a webhook.
- pure API calls (no browser, no local file work, no calendar/email orchestration). The manager handles pod-aware auth, multi-step flows (createSession → use token → next call), and result chaining.
- when the task explicitly names a service with an API (Bluesky / Mastodon / Reddit / GitHub / Spotify / WHOOP / OpenAI / Anthropic / Polymarket / OnStar / etc.) and the user wants to act on that service.

++ sandbox_manager
- Python in a Docker sandbox: compute / data analysis, image / PDF / document processing, OCR, file transformations, generating charts or reports.
- Use when the task says "resize / convert / extract / transform / analyze / compute / generate" and there's a file, dataset, or computation involved.
- Requires AUTH_USER — only user-initiated.
- NOT for HTTP API calls (route to http_manager) or browser actions (playwright_manager) or simple file listing (bash_manager).

++ emi_team_manager
- mixed cross-domain work that combines web + admin + other domains.
- generalist manager that can call other managers, particularly good with pods.
- "my <external service>" tasks that need to combine identity/KG context with multiple downstream actions.
- **For pure API calls on a single service, prefer http_manager** — emi_team_manager is for compound flows (e.g. "find a post on Bluesky AND send the link to Katy by email" mixes http + email).
- This is the manager to call when you are unsure what to call.

++ devices_manager
- smart-home control intents focused on Nest thermostat, lights, and Ring cameras.
- use this when smart-home work is multi-step, ambiguous, or needs planning/coordination.

++ bash_manager
- local filesystem work on the user's machine: list folders / files, find a file by name, show contents of a text file, mint a pod from a file.
- read intents only: "list my Documents", "what's in my Pictures", "find a file called X", "show me the contents of Y".
- For compound tasks that involve sending the file somewhere (email, etc.), route to emi_team_manager — it orchestrates bash + personal_admin. bash_manager itself does NOT send.

++ run_task — run a pre-made task, routine, or job by name.
- When user says "run task X", "run job X", "run routine X", "execute task X".
- Put only the task/routine name in `task` (e.g. `timesheets`), not a full sentence.

++ task_compile_manager — compile a task spec into workflow IR.
- Choose this ONLY when compile intent is explicit (user says "compile").
- Requests to edit/update/rewrite task spec content are NOT compile by default.

++ Everything else/uncertain -> emi_team_manager

